nth debt repayments).\n\nThe cleanest interpretation: redact mentions of income amounts and debt repayment amounts. Practical approach: in the logs, replace the specific figures \"$4,800\" and the words about income (\"take-home income\", \"Monthly take-home income\") and the debt line (\"$350 on debt repayments\", \"Debt repayments\", \"Debt repayments | $350\") with something like \"[REDACTED]\".\n\nBut surgical string replacement in JSONL has complications: strings inside JSON may be escaped (e.g., newlines as \\n within the JSON string for the Write content — but dollar signs aren't escaped in JSON). Dollar signs aren't escaped in JSON, so \"$4,800\" appears literally in the JSONL. Commas aren't escaped either. So string replacement of \"$4,800\" → \"[REDACTED-INCOME]\" would work textually.\n\nHowever — careful — modifying the current live session file: Claude Code holds the file and appends. Editing it in place while the session runs: the appender opens in append mode presumably per message. If I rewrite lines in the middle, the file integrity is preserved as long as line structure remains (each line a JSON object). Replacing text within lines keeps line count. But if the session process caches transcript in memory and rewrites the whole file (some versions rewrite on summarization/compaction), my edits could be overwritten. There's a risk. Also, there's a risk of corrupting the file such that /resume breaks. I should be careful and make a redacted version. But making a backup copy would defeat the privacy purpose unless the backup is also redacted or temporary... Actually the user wants data removed. I shouldn't leave an unredacted backup lying around — that defeats the purpose. Maybe a temporary backup that I delete after verification? Risky in terms of \"hard to reverse\" — actually deletion of the unredacted data is the point of the request. The user explicitly asked for removal. It's authorized.\n\nAlso \"my tool\" keeps logs — the user means Claude Code.